  "kind": "game",
  "title": "Pocky & Rocky 2",
  "subtitle": "Natsume · Super Nintendo Entertainment System",
  "summary": "Pocky & Rocky 2 is Natsume's Super Nintendo action sequel, sending Pocky on another adventure through monsters and places drawn from Japanese folklore. She moves freely around stages viewed from above, throwing talismans at enemies and travelling with a supporting companion. A second player can take control of that companion, with different allies providing their own attacks and abilities. The sequel changes the balance of the partnership. Pocky remains the central traveller, while the person beside her becomes a changing set of useful possibilities.",
  "body": [
    "Pocky & Rocky 2 is Natsume's Super Nintendo action sequel, sending Pocky on another adventure through monsters and places drawn from Japanese folklore. She moves freely around stages viewed from above, throwing talismans at enemies and travelling with a supporting companion. A second player can take control of that companion, with different allies providing their own attacks and abilities. The sequel changes the balance of the partnership. Pocky remains the central traveller, while the person beside her becomes a changing set of useful possibilities.",
    "Pocky can throw a partner toward an enemy for a powerful attack or temporarily take on the partner's abilities through magic. Choosing an ally therefore affects how an obstacle can be approached. In single-player, the computer handles the companion; a second player can instead provide direct assistance. The supporting role has unlimited lives, creating a different relationship from the equal vulnerability of the first game's two characters. The pair still share the scene, but the sequel gives their cooperation a more explicitly organised purpose.",
    "Attack upgrades and protective clothing help Pocky survive the successive stages, while another companion can alter the rhythm of a familiar encounter. The visual world retains its animated creatures and folklore imagery, making the expanded cast feel at home beside her. Released in Japan in 1994, with overseas editions following, the sequel develops cooperation by giving the second character a distinct role. Its interest lies in what the partnership makes possible: the same central hero can answer a new situation differently because somebody else is travelling alongside her."
  ],
  "story_headings": [
    "A shrine maiden brings more company",
    "The companion is part of the toolset",
    "A different way to share the screen"
